Autoimmune Dysautonomia
Complex Fatigue Entities
Antibody-mediated autonomic failure

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-003
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe orthostatic intolerance and systemic weakness to rule out cardiogenic syncope or severe dehydration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage highlighted recurrent near-syncope upon standing, severe nausea, and profound fatigue, creating an immediate fall risk and concern for acute hypoperfusion. On-site assessment was critical to evaluate hemodynamic stability and prevent syncopal trauma prior to regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every time I stand up the room spins, my stomach churns, and I just can't get out of bed.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive 3-day exacerbation of severe orthostatic hypotension, GI dysmotility, and pervasive fatigue. Timeline indicates increasing dizziness upon standing, followed by early satiety, severe bloating, and nausea preventing adequate fluid intake. The fatigue is described as leaden and unyielding.
Functional Status: Bedbound today due to high risk of syncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: Reliant on adult child for fluid provision; extremely high fall risk if ambulating to the toilet unassisted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 supine, 85/55 standing mmHg | HR: 75 supine, 105 standing bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Pale, fatigued, visibly lethargic. Conversant but slow to respond.
Physical Exam: Positive orthostatic vital signs indicating severe hemodynamic shift. Cardiovascular exam reveals regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs. Abdomen is distended but soft, with sluggish bowel sounds. Pupils equal and reactive. Clinical picture represents antibody-mediated autonomic failure.
Environmental Observations: Dimly lit room, significant clutter around the bed path increasing trip hazards during presyncopal episodes.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient ceased regular multivitamin due to nausea. No current hypotensive agents identified.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Autoimmune Dysautonomia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute viral gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of diarrhea or fever, and chronicity of underlying autonomic symptoms.

Cardiogenic syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal auscultation, gradual symptom onset linked precisely to postural changes rather than exertional or sudden arrhythmic events.

Severe clinical dehydration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Moist mucous membranes and adequate capillary refill despite the postural blood pressure drop.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The profound drop in standing blood pressure accompanied by compensatory tachycardia and pre-syncope mandated immediate after-hours evaluation. The clinical presentation presented an imminent risk of syncope-induced traumatic injury and required urgent differentiation from life-threatening cardiogenic or hemorrhagic causes of hypotension. Immediate in-home evaluation was necessary to institute strict positional management, provide antiemetic therapy to secure oral hydration, and prevent inevitable hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered sublingual Ondansetron 4mg to manage GI dysmotility and facilitate oral rehydration. Prescribed oral rehydration salts and increased dietary sodium. Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN, traditional IVIG pending specialist, and NAD+. Recommended tilt-table testing and autoimmune autonomic serology.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically drinking 500ml of electrolyte fluid before getting out of bed, you will artificially expand your blood volume, directly preventing the dizzy spells and rewarding yourself with safe, stable mobility.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely required.
DME/Safety Optional: Shower chair recommended to prevent falls during high-risk ADLs.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours for specialist neurology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to ED if patient experiences actual loss of consciousness, intractable vomiting preventing any fluid intake for 12 hours, or new onset chest pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a complex neuro-immunological presentation requiring specialized tertiary investigations to confirm the exact antibody etiology.
Clinical Course: Autonomic flares are exhausting but generally self-limiting with strict conservative management of fluid volume and gradual positional changes.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 if you pass out completely, hit your head, or feel crushing chest pressure.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The anti-nausea medication allows you to drink the required salt fluids, which are critical to pushing your blood pressure back up to safe levels.
Actionable Steps: Sit on the edge of the bed for a full two minutes before standing; this deliberate pause gives your nervous system time to adapt, physically preventing the blood from dropping out of your brain.

